.TH CJSON 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cjson \- overview of the JSON parser, serializer and DOM
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
cjson parses JSON text (RFC 8259) into a tree of nodes, which a
program can read and change, and then writes the tree back as JSON.
Each node is identified by an opaque cjson handle.
.fi
.SH "NODE TYPES"
.nf
cjson_type(3) gives the type of a node, as a cjson_node_type_t:

  CJSON_NULL         null
  CJSON_BOOL         true or false, read with cjson_bool_val(3)
  CJSON_INTEGER      a number with no '.' and no exponent that fits a
                     long long, read with cjson_int_val(3)
  CJSON_FLOAT        all other numbers, a double, read with
                     cjson_double_val(3)
  CJSON_STRING       a UTF-8 string, read with cjson_str_val(3)
  CJSON_LIST         an array of child nodes
  CJSON_DICTIONARY   an object: string keys, each with a child node

A value accessor stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when the
node has a different type, and also for a NULL node.  So call
cjson_type(3) before you read a node whose type comes from the input.
cjson_parse(3) gives the rules for large integers, strings and UTF-8.
.fi
.SH "PATHS"
.nf
cjson_get(3), cjson_set(3) and cjson_delete(3) find a node through a
string of dot-separated components, for example "users.#0.name".  On
an array, a component "#N" is the element at index N; every other
component is an object key.  Write "\e." for a dot in a key and "\e\e"
for a backslash.  cjson_set(3) creates a missing object key at the
leaf, but it never creates a missing parent, and never an array
element after the end.
.fi
.SH "ORDER"
.nf
An object keeps its members in insertion order:
  - For a parsed document, the order of the text.
  - For a tree that a program makes, the order of the calls that added
    the keys.
A replacement keeps the position of its key.  The serializers,
cjson_clone(3) and the cursor of cjson_dictionary_first(3) all use
this order, so the output for one tree is the same in each run.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP"
.nf
cjson_parse(3), cjson_clone(3) and the cjson_create_*(3) functions give
a tree that the caller owns and must free with cjson_destroy(3).

cjson_get(3), cjson_list_get(3), cjson_dictionary_get(3) and the
dictionary cursor give borrowed references, which the tree continues
to own.  To attach such a node at a different location, clone it
first.

cjson_list_push(3) and cjson_dictionary_set(3) take the child; only
ccol_invalid_args leaves the child with the caller.

Each node records its allocator, so destroying a tree needs no
allocator from the caller.  A string from cjson_serialize(3) is freed
by the allocator of the root.
.fi
.SH "LIMITS"
.nf
A parse refuses nesting deeper than 500 containers, and the
serializers and cjson_clone(3) refuse it too for a tree that a program
makes.  The size of a parsed tree is linear in the length of the
input; cjson_parse(3) gives the limit.  A string never holds a null
byte, because each string is a null-terminated C string.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A tree is not thread safe, but many threads can read one tree at the
same time while no thread changes it.  Separate trees are independent,
and separate threads can parse and serialize at the same time.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
